        "source_level": "B1",
        "word_count": 91,
        "duration_sec": null,
        "body_text": "Young people are having less sex. What's going on? - video Sex among British adults has slumped in recent decades, with under-45s having half as many monthly encounters as in 1990 and one in three under-25s reporting that they have never had sexual intercourse.",
        "audio_url": null,
        "description": null,
        "license": "proprietary",
        "attribution": null,
        "truncated": true,
        "guid_hash": "3dcf2314040fe4d3354833ec8a8961496ee74e32",
        "summary": "Sex among British adults has slumped in recent decades, with under-45s having half as many monthly encounters as in 1990 and one in three under-25s reporting that they have never had sexual intercourse. To unpick some of the possible reasons behind these declines, Madeleine Finlay hears from science editor and co-host Ian Sample. He tells her why young people might be having sex later, and what could account for the striking rise in women reporting that they don’t want children.",
